{%- set deployIntro %}
<p class="mx-auto mt-4 max-w-2xl text-lg">
  Deploying it yourself means renting a server, pasting eight commands into it once, and then working in a
  browser. Join everyone who ran <code>docker compose up</code>. We don't count.
</p>
<p class="mx-auto mt-2 max-w-2xl text-sm">
  Below is the README's Quick start: eight commands, pasted into a terminal on the server. The first one,
  <code>git clone</code>, downloads the project's source folder. Docker Engine 25+
  with Compose 2.20+. (The network name comes from the maintainer's own Traefik setup; you don't need n8n.)
</p>
{% endset %}
{%- set deployCmd %}{% include "quickstart-cmd.njk" %}{% endset %}
{%- set deployNotes %}
<p class="mx-auto mt-2 max-w-2xl text-sm">
  Run it once: pasting it again inside the install folder clones a second copy and restarts on its empty
  database, which signs everyone out (to recover, <code>cd</code> back to the original folder and run
  <code>docker compose up -d</code>). Then open <code>http://127.0.0.1:3001/?t=&lt;SHARE_TOKEN&gt;</code> (the token is
  in <code>.env</code>; from your own computer, forward the port first with
  <code>ssh -L 3001:127.0.0.1:3001 you@your-server</code>) and register the owner at <code>/login</code>.
  Each chatter signs in at <code>/chatter-login</code>. Before chatters reach it from outside, read the
  <a class="text-link" href="{{ '/help/#security' | url }}">security notes</a>: today it belongs on a private
  network or a VPN, not on the open internet.
</p>
{% endset %}
{% include "deploy-band.njk" %}
